20:Keywords: Corpus callosum Major depressive disorder VBM First-episode depression meta-analysis
24:Major depressive disorder (MDD) is among the most common psychiatric  conditions  worldwide  and  this  severe  mental  condition  is characterized  by  a  high  risk  of  recurrence  and  a  high  probability  of chronicity (Villalobos et al., 2021). MDD is characterized by high variability  in  clinical  symptoms and has been documented to result in a series  of  neurobiological  changes  in  the  brain,  including  structural changes as in the early phases of the depressive state (e.g. Schmaal et al., 2017), on the other hand, adolescent first episode MDD patients do not seem  to  show  changes  in  brain  volume  compared  to  age-matched healthy controls (Schmaal et al., 2016), suggesting that structural (and functional)  changes  in  the  brain  due  to  MDD  may  appear  later  in
30:Methods: To further investigate this question, we compared 369 first-episode MDD patients (mean age = 35 years (sd = 12), 249 females; 283 early onset, 86 mid-to-late onset) from the open-source REST meta-MDD database closely matched for age and gender to 490 never-depressed individuals (mean age = 37 years (sd = 14); 309 females) using Z -scores obtained from normative neuroanatomical modeling to assess individual variability in CC (sub)volumes.
32:Results: Relative  to  the  norms  established  by  the  healthy  controls,  first-episode  MDD  patients  displayed  CC volume (z-score) reductions in the entire CC (including the body), as did mid-to-late-onset first-episode MDD patients (age ≥ 45 y). In early-onset first-episode MDD patients (age ≤ 44 y), depression severity symptoms were related to volume increases in the entire CC, as well as the body and splenium.
48:journal homepage: www.elsevier.com/locate/jad
50:![Image](./Wu2023_artifacts/image_000003_9f805e525236729c06f0c093beea075c7108c076596e6bbbb4e2f33162be9645.png)
52:![Image](./Wu2023_artifacts/image_000004_e2461357d2bc3b5460075b08d1fe2ca2a0883b107ea416740c6fc6a962ccbf4d.png)
54:G.-R. Wu and C. Baeken hemispheric  activity  by  connecting,  transmitting  information,  and integrating  neural  activity  within/between  both  hemispheres  (Ran et al., 2020). As such, the CC is believed to be involved in the hemispheric communication of higher cognitive functions, among which are attention, arousal, language, emotion, and memory to name just a few (Hahn et al., 2015). Whereas in neurological literature, the CC has been put  forward  as  a  keynote  player  involved  in  the  pathophysiology  of several major neurological conditions, including multiple sclerosis and Alzheimer's disease (Chakraborty and Pal, 2019), in adult MDD however, the CC has rarely been put central in structural brain research in psychiatry. The few available studies focusing on CC volumes, mostly including  small  clinical  samples,  have  produced  rather  unequivocal findings (Cheng et al., 2010; Lee et al., 2020; Ozalay et al., 2013; Ran et al., 2020), yielding no clear direction (see Supplemental Table 1 and meta-analytical approach Supplemental Fig. 1). Here it must be added that these studies were not uniform in the used atlases/CC parcelations. In spite of the fact that the evidence is not overwhelming, it is broadly assumed that MDD results in CC volume decreases, predominantly in the prefrontal (genu) parts (Wise et al., 2016). However, it remains to be clarified  whether  such  CC  volumetric  changes  already  occur  when experiencing  a  first  depressive  episode.  Furthermore,  given  the  high variability in depressive symptomatology, more individualized analytical approaches may be more suitable (Rutherford et al., 2022).
56:Therefore, we investigated CC (sub)volume differences (comprising genu, body, splenium) between first episode MDD patients and age and gender  matched  never-depressed  healthy  controls.  Because  heterogeneity is an important aspect of MDD in symptoms and CC volumes, we examined how CC (sub)volumes in MDD can be parameterized as an individualized metric of matched never-depressed normal controls. We applied  normative  modeling  resulting  in  individualized  CC  (sub)volumes in MDD. We hypothesized that besides the entire CC especially the genu  would  show  volume  decreases.  We  further  examined  whether experiencing an 'early ' or 'mid-to-late ' first depressive episode would affect  CC  (sub)volume differently.  Finally,  we  also  explored  whether depression severity symptoms could affect CC (sub)volumes.
58:## 2. Materials and methods
